  summary: "The Muslim sultanates of the Ethiopian region arose from the growth of long-distance trade and the spread of Islam along the Zeyla routes, where Muslim merchants peacefully converted local communities. The most important were Shewa, Ifat, Fatagar, Dawaro, Darha, Bali, Hadya, Arababni, Sharkha and Adal, and they shared a Muslim population, rulers with religious and political power and an economy based mainly on trade. Rivalry over the Zeyla trade set them against the Christian highland kingdom, and after Ifat fell the Walasma founded Adal, which later became the leading Muslim power.",
